  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 PM EDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nVery few changes were made to the forecast today as a persistence \nforecast was largely utilized through the weekend. A large area of \nsurface high pressure remains over the Northeastern US extending \nback across the Great Lakes. With amplified upper level ridging over \nthe central CONUS building into the Great Lakes through the weekend, \na tranquil week is ahead. Expect lots of sunshine, light winds, and \nhighs generally in the upper 60s to low 70s each day. Sunday will be \nthe warmest day of the week with the upper level ridge centered \noverhead and highs in the low to mid 70s. Little to no rainfall is \nexpected over the next 7 days; We could see some showers out of a \ncold frontal passage early next week late Monday night into Tuesday \nbut for now chances remain very low (15-20%) as it remains to be \nseen if there will be sufficient forcing and moisture \navailable. Towards the middle of next week, temperature \ngenerally trend towards normal with highs around 60 degrees as \nthe aforementioned synoptic pattern breaks down. \n\nLocally dense fog is possible again overnight into Thursday morning, \nespecially in northeast Indiana and northwest Ohio along and south \nof US 24. Light N/NE flow may allow for advection fog to develop \nagain. Forecast soundings indicate potential for small dewpoint \ndepressions as temperatures and dewpoints are both expected to be in \nthe low 40s tonight. Much like last night, the best timeframe for \ndense fog will be between 3 AM to 11 AM EDT Thursday, with the \nlowest visibilities around daybreak. A SPS or Dense Fog Advisory may \nbe needed overnight depending on trends and where any fog\ndevelops.",
